WEBVTT

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:15.450
They say this painting was originally

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.530
Episode 7

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.530
THE PRETTY BOY IN THE ATTIC

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.530
Part 2

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.540
THE BIG KIDNAPPING OF THE BIG PAINTING

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.460
a painting of all Yubiwa Academy
Middle School students

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.790
who conducted the general student meeting.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.910
NAGAHIRO 
SAKIGUCHI

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.290
However...

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:30.090
...seven years ago, every student
who was painted here was kidnapped

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.970
by the artist, Ms. Towai.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:40.390
In other words, Ms. Towai switched
the painting to one with no students,

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:43.310
and to an empty one.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.690
I'd think that people faced with

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:49.320
a theft of this grand scale
would file for damages.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.240
The reason why Yubiwa Academy

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.870
didn't file for damages against
the crime Ms. Towai committed

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:59.580
was because that grand kidnapping
was an impossible crime.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:02.710
Impossible crime?

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.460
MAYUMI 
DOJIMA

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:06.590
Well, isn't that an exaggeration?

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.090
Because what Ms. Towai actually did
was simply swap out a painting...

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:14.550
Simply?

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.350
Simply?

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.430
This painting?

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:19.980
Sakiguchi.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:24.350
This painting is a lot larger
than the auditorium entrance.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:28.530
How in the world did
Ms. Towai swap this out?

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.110
SHIP IN A BOTTLE

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:35.570
For instance,
a sailboat that's placed within a bottle.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.740
A sailboat that's bigger
than the bottleneck

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.660
is placed inside as broken pieces,

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.540
and constructed inside the bottle.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:48.550
Of course, Ms. Towai did the same when she
painted The Auditorium in the Auditorium.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.960
She brought in the materials separately,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.140
and started by constructing
the canvas inside the auditorium.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:00.180
But she can't use that method
when switching the painting out.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.980
That act would imply criminality,
so it cannot be caught in the public eye.

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:07.060
It's impossible.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.650
It's an impossible crime.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.240
MANABU 
SOTOIN

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.320
How wonderful, Nagahiro.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.160
To think you were hiding
such an ace up your sleeve.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.370
I'm sure things would be easier if we
could've spoken directly with Ms. Towai.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.240
HYOTA
 ASHIKAGA

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.960
To ask for the answer directly
from the artist is not something

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.670
a detective should be doing.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:34.680
In my opinion, a perpetrator's confession
does not count as evidence.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.720
Of course, if we're verifying our answers,

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.680
I would be willing to listen
to Lady Towai's story.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.520
Nagahiro. Knowing you,

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.230
the whereabouts of Lady Towai
is already under investigation, right?

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.270
When can you find her?

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.280
I believe we will know
by tomorrow morning.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:51.400
SOSAKU
 YUBIWA

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:56.570
I see. Then, let's all gather here
early tomorrow morning.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:00.990
Everyone, make sure you find
the truth by then.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:02.450
That's your homework!

